    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d165-a1">

                <pb facs="0330" n="234" xml:id="pg234"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 2 in No.
                    81.—Translation.]</seg></opener>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Red letter of the Yamên to
                        the Dean of the diplomatic corps.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1898-10-04"><hi rendition="#italic">October 4,
                        1898</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>Referring to the incident of brick and stone throwing at Tien-ciao, the Yamên has
                    already given orders for the arrest and severe punishment of the offenders and
                    to establish certain measures of protection in order to avoid troubles which may
                    follow.</p>
                <p>Just now we have heard that all the foreign ministers who reside at Peking have
                    spoken of a project to call troops to Peking for the purpose of guarding
                    themselves.</p>
                <p>If this is true, we believe the popular sentiment will be excited by it and it
                    will be likely to produce new incidents.</p>
                <p>In a word, China must avow absolutely the right which she has to protect, and to
                    find means to that end without inconsideration or hurry.</p>
                <p>We must therefore beg your excellency to be kind enough to request the ministers,
                    your colleagues, to telegraph in order to stop the soldiers, whom it is not
                    necessary to call to Peking.</p>
                <p>Please accept, etc.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
